1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,316
Letters with Money:

2
00:00:01,396 --> 00:00:06,100
Singapore's Remittance Houses and
the Savings Sent Home to China

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:09,292
For more than a century,
a Chinese labourer in

4
00:00:09,372 --> 00:00:12,565
Singapore who wanted to
send part of his wages

5
00:00:12,645 --> 00:00:15,775
to his family in China
did not go to a bank.

6
00:00:15,775 --> 00:00:19,003
He went to a remittance
house, often a tea merchant,

7
00:00:19,083 --> 00:00:23,011
a medicine hall or a grocer in
the back streets near the river,

8
00:00:23,091 --> 00:00:27,401
which took his money and his letter,
sent them to his home village in

9
00:00:27,481 --> 00:00:31,425
Fujian or Guangdong, and weeks
later brought him back a reply.

10
00:00:31,425 --> 00:00:35,395
The letter that travelled with
the money was called a qiaopi,

11
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:38,183
and Singapore was the hub of the trade for

12
00:00:38,263 --> 00:00:41,901
the whole of Southeast Asia,
with more remittance houses

13
00:00:41,981 --> 00:00:45,300
at one point than the rest
of the region combined.

14
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,631
When the colonial government tried
to take the business over in 1876,

15
00:00:50,711 --> 00:00:54,475
it set off one of the
town's most serious riots.

16
00:00:54,475 --> 00:00:58,089
Singapore's early Chinese
settlers came mostly from the

17
00:00:58,169 --> 00:01:01,716
coasts of Fujian and Guangdong,
and almost all of them

18
00:01:01,796 --> 00:01:05,625
had families at home who
depended on what they sent back.

19
00:01:05,625 --> 00:01:10,057
In 1847 the merchant Seah
Eu Chin described the season

20
00:01:10,137 --> 00:01:14,900
for remittances in the Journal
of the Indian Archipelago.

21
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,587
Each year, when the junks were
about to sail back to China,

22
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:21,588
the streets of the business
quarter filled with

23
00:01:21,668 --> 00:01:25,547
labourers from the gambier and
pepper plantations who had come

24
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:29,075
into town to send their
annual letters and money home.

25
00:01:29,075 --> 00:01:32,155
Some gave their savings
to a friend going home,

26
00:01:32,235 --> 00:01:36,862
but most used a man who made a
business of it, travelling on the junks

27
00:01:36,942 --> 00:01:40,775
with letters and money from
people from his own district.

28
00:01:40,775 --> 00:01:44,040
He was paid a commission
of ten per cent, or he was

29
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:47,844
allowed to buy goods with the
money, sell them in China at

30
00:01:47,924 --> 00:01:51,400
his own risk, and pay the
family out of the proceeds.

31
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:55,987
By Seah's estimate the total
sent in a year ranged from about

32
00:01:56,067 --> 00:02:00,501
30,000 to 70,000 Spanish dollars,
depending on trade and on

33
00:02:00,581 --> 00:02:05,325
the price of gambier, which set
the plantation workers' wages.

34
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:09,957
These couriers came to be called
shuike, or "water guests",

35
00:02:10,037 --> 00:02:14,350
and by 1849 there were about
200 of them in Singapore.

36
00:02:14,350 --> 00:02:17,425
Many of the labourers could not write.

37
00:02:17,425 --> 00:02:21,001
Seah described the public
letter-writers who sat at the

38
00:02:21,081 --> 00:02:25,189
side of the street, in the
verandahs or in the shade of a wall,

39
00:02:25,269 --> 00:02:28,114
with a small rough
table, a bundle of paper,

40
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:32,050
a brush and ink, writing down
what each customer dictated.

41
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:35,945
At the height of the season, he
wrote, forty to fifty of these

42
00:02:36,025 --> 00:02:40,000
stalls could be counted along
the streets in a single morning.

43
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,862
In later years, Singapore's
letter-writers typically charged

44
00:02:43,942 --> 00:02:47,425
three to six cents a letter,
depending on its length.

45
00:02:47,425 --> 00:02:51,980
From the 1830s, some merchants
with capital to spare began

46
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:56,775
setting up remittance houses,
known in Chinese as qiaopiju.

47
00:02:56,775 --> 00:02:59,579
Most were sidelines of other businesses:

48
00:02:59,659 --> 00:03:03,832
tea merchants, medicine halls,
printers, bakers and grocers

49
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:07,300
took in remittances
alongside their main trade.

50
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,100
Each house served its own people.

51
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:14,175
A Hokkien labourer used a Hokkien
house and a Hainanese labourer

52
00:03:14,255 --> 00:03:18,200
a Hainanese one, partly because
he trusted people from his own

53
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:22,745
district and partly because he could
speak to them in his own dialect,

54
00:03:22,825 --> 00:03:26,850
and the clerks would write his
letter for him if he needed it.

55
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:31,381
The larger houses had a head
office in Singapore and branches in

56
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:36,000
Hong Kong and in the towns near
their customers' home villages.

57
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,925
A collector went round the plantations and

58
00:03:39,005 --> 00:03:42,225
settlements gathering
letters and small sums.

59
00:03:42,225 --> 00:03:45,893
The letters were made up into
a single "clubbed" packet

60
00:03:45,973 --> 00:03:49,028
and sent to the branch
in China, and the money

61
00:03:49,108 --> 00:03:52,925
followed by bank draft or,
in the early years, as goods.

62
00:03:52,925 --> 00:03:56,238
In China, the branch
delivered each letter and its

63
00:03:56,318 --> 00:03:59,766
money to the family's door,
and the family sent back

64
00:03:59,846 --> 00:04:03,375
a short reply, the huipi,
which served as a receipt.

65
00:04:03,375 --> 00:04:07,850
A collector usually made three
or four round trips a year.

66
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:10,764
The houses made money less from their fees

67
00:04:10,844 --> 00:04:13,625
than from holding the money in between.

68
00:04:13,625 --> 00:04:17,543
The historian Lane J. Harris
has shown how, by the 1870s,

69
00:04:17,623 --> 00:04:20,910
they used the remittances
as short-term capital,

70
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:25,188
buying goods in Southeast Asia
to sell in Hong Kong or China,

71
00:04:25,268 --> 00:04:30,378
or dealing in the exchange rates between
the currencies of Southeast Asia,

72
00:04:30,458 --> 00:04:33,825
Hong Kong and China,
before paying the families.

73
00:04:33,825 --> 00:04:36,559
The return receipt protected the sender,

74
00:04:36,639 --> 00:04:39,875
since it confirmed that
the money had arrived.

75
00:04:39,875 --> 00:04:44,200
The colonial government took a
growing interest in the trade.

76
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:48,703
From 1872 the Postmaster-General,
Henry Trotter, argued that the

77
00:04:48,783 --> 00:04:53,358
private remittance houses were
infringing the government's postal

78
00:04:53,438 --> 00:04:57,950
monopoly and taking revenue that
should have gone to the state.

79
00:04:57,950 --> 00:05:01,824
Two Straits-born Chinese
remittance merchants from Penang,

80
00:05:01,904 --> 00:05:04,073
Ong Kong Chang and Ong Kong Teng,

81
00:05:04,153 --> 00:05:07,425
offered to run the business
for the Post Office.

82
00:05:07,425 --> 00:05:11,307
In 1876 Governor Sir
William Jervois made them

83
00:05:11,387 --> 00:05:15,183
salaried sub-postmasters
and arranged to open

84
00:05:15,263 --> 00:05:19,225
a Chinese Sub-Post Office
at 81 Market Street.

85
00:05:19,225 --> 00:05:23,653
Under the scheme, any Chinese firm
could still collect remittances,

86
00:05:23,733 --> 00:05:26,749
but letters had to be
stamped and sent through

87
00:05:26,829 --> 00:05:30,786
the government's sub-post office
or the General Post Office,

88
00:05:30,866 --> 00:05:34,500
at a fixed scale of charges,
by way of Swatow or Amoy.

89
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,401
The remittance merchants
read this as an attack

90
00:05:37,481 --> 00:05:40,128
on their business,
since it took away their

91
00:05:40,208 --> 00:05:43,125
control over how and
when the money travelled.

92
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:47,451
Placards went up around the town
claiming that all letters would

93
00:05:47,531 --> 00:05:51,789
now have to go through the
government, and one offered a reward

94
00:05:51,869 --> 00:05:56,000
of 100 taels for the heads of
the two "Post Office farmers".

95
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,505
On the morning of the
15th of December 1876,

96
00:05:59,585 --> 00:06:04,800
the day the sub-post office opened,
a crowd gathered outside it.

97
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,353
Someone threw a brick, and the office

98
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:10,350
was wrecked and the royal arms torn down.

99
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:15,091
The rioters then attacked the police
station at the Ellenborough New Market,

100
00:06:15,171 --> 00:06:18,850
where the police fired on the
crowd, killing three people.

101
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:22,249
The Superintendent of
Police, R. W. Maxwell,

102
00:06:22,329 --> 00:06:25,650
was stoned, knocked down and badly beaten.

103
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:30,625
Forty rioters were arrested, and
most were caned and imprisoned.

104
00:06:30,625 --> 00:06:34,659
That afternoon, soldiers of the
80th Regiment took the towkays

105
00:06:34,739 --> 00:06:39,172
who had run the letter and remittance
business and put them on board

106
00:06:39,252 --> 00:06:43,300
the government steamer Pluto,
three miles out in the harbour.

107
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:47,200
The next day the Chinese
shops closed in protest.

108
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:50,044
The towkays were brought
back two days later,

109
00:06:50,124 --> 00:06:54,787
after agreeing to give bonds for their
good behaviour, and the government

110
00:06:54,867 --> 00:06:58,296
deported Lim Ah Tye, the
head of the Teochew branch of

111
00:06:58,376 --> 00:07:02,275
the Ghee Hin secret society,
whom it blamed for the trouble.

112
00:07:02,275 --> 00:07:07,325
On Monday the 18th of December
the sub-post office reopened.

113
00:07:07,325 --> 00:07:09,571
At the time, officials and the

114
00:07:09,651 --> 00:07:12,675
newspapers blamed the secret societies.

115
00:07:12,675 --> 00:07:16,257
Harris argues that the
remittance merchants themselves,

116
00:07:16,337 --> 00:07:19,653
using the secret societies
to organise the protest,

117
00:07:19,733 --> 00:07:23,116
were defending their way
of doing business against a

118
00:07:23,196 --> 00:07:26,925
state that did not understand
how they made their money.

119
00:07:26,925 --> 00:07:30,975
The government did not drive
the remittance houses out.

120
00:07:30,975 --> 00:07:36,193
In 1877 the Chinese Sub-Post Office
moved into the General Post Office,

121
00:07:36,273 --> 00:07:40,521
and over the following years
the private houses came under

122
00:07:40,601 --> 00:07:45,600
the Post Office's supervision while
carrying on their own business.

123
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:49,868
Their clubbed packets of letters
were sent through the post,

124
00:07:49,948 --> 00:07:54,875
with the postage cut from six cents
to three cents a letter in 1888.

125
00:07:54,875 --> 00:07:58,075
The number of letters climbed steadily.

126
00:07:58,075 --> 00:08:03,500
In 1887 Singapore had 49
Chinese letter offices.

127
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:07,258
Thirty-four of them were
Teochew and eleven Hokkien,

128
00:08:07,338 --> 00:08:12,800
and the first Cantonese, Hainanese and
Hakka offices had opened that year.

129
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:16,525
The trade was not without
risk for the senders.

130
00:08:16,525 --> 00:08:21,123
Two houses failed in 1890 and
1891, and two more in 1907

131
00:08:21,203 --> 00:08:24,131
after gambling on the exchange rate,

132
00:08:24,211 --> 00:08:29,975
losing money that labourers had
entrusted to them for their families.

133
00:08:29,975 --> 00:08:34,709
By the early twentieth century,
according to the historian Zhang Huimei,

134
00:08:34,789 --> 00:08:37,518
Singapore had about 200 remittance houses,

135
00:08:37,598 --> 00:08:42,399
more than the rest of Southeast Asia
combined, and letters and money from

136
00:08:42,479 --> 00:08:46,825
across the region passed through
Singapore on their way to China.

137
00:08:46,825 --> 00:08:52,134
By 1937 there were 42 Hokkien
houses of various sizes,

138
00:08:52,214 --> 00:08:55,228
and more than 50 Teochew houses

139
00:08:55,308 --> 00:08:59,200
had been set up in the 1920s and 1930s.

140
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,950
The Pacific War cut the link.

141
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:06,656
From December 1941 letters and
money could no longer reach China,

142
00:09:06,736 --> 00:09:09,601
and although the Japanese administration

143
00:09:09,681 --> 00:09:13,944
allowed some remittances through
Taiwan and Japanese banks,

144
00:09:14,024 --> 00:09:18,000
very few people used the
route and it was soon closed.

145
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,135
Families in China who
depended on money from

146
00:09:21,215 --> 00:09:24,650
Singapore went without
for the rest of the war.

147
00:09:24,650 --> 00:09:28,350
After the war came the
trade's golden years.

148
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:34,197
In November 1945 the banks and remittance
houses agreed an exchange rate,

149
00:09:34,277 --> 00:09:37,850
and a flood of delayed
remittances followed.

150
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:44,152
In March 1946 the houses formed the
Nanyang Chinese Remittance Association,

151
00:09:44,232 --> 00:09:47,131
and by the late 1940s, at the peak,

152
00:09:47,211 --> 00:09:51,125
Singapore had more than
200 remittance houses.

153
00:09:51,125 --> 00:09:55,850
The colonial government, however,
now controlled foreign exchange.

154
00:09:55,850 --> 00:09:59,700
From 1946 remittances to
China had to go through

155
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:03,465
banks or approved agents,
and one family could

156
00:10:03,545 --> 00:10:07,475
receive no more than 45
Straits dollars a month.

157
00:10:07,475 --> 00:10:12,091
From October 1948 the remittance
houses had to be licensed,

158
00:10:12,171 --> 00:10:17,185
with a deposit of $10,000 for a
house that sent letters directly

159
00:10:17,265 --> 00:10:22,200
to China and $2,000 for one that
worked through another house.

160
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:25,602
After the founding of
the People's Republic

161
00:10:25,682 --> 00:10:29,325
of China in 1949, the
business changed again.

162
00:10:29,325 --> 00:10:34,641
A stable currency in China took away
much of the profit from exchange dealing,

163
00:10:34,721 --> 00:10:38,792
the new government cracked down
on black-market remittances,

164
00:10:38,872 --> 00:10:43,300
and fewer overseas Chinese wanted
to invest in property at home.

165
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:48,225
More slowly, Singapore's Chinese
population was changing too.

166
00:10:48,225 --> 00:10:50,714
More of them were born in Singapore,

167
00:10:50,794 --> 00:10:54,575
with families of their own
here rather than in China.

168
00:10:54,575 --> 00:10:58,523
By the 1970s about 60
remittance houses were left,

169
00:10:58,603 --> 00:11:02,792
and the trade faded away
from the late 1970s as banks

170
00:11:02,872 --> 00:11:06,900
and the post office took
over what remained of it.

171
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:11,582
The letters themselves survived
in huge numbers, kept by families

172
00:11:11,662 --> 00:11:16,350
in China and later gathered by
archives, collectors and museums.

173
00:11:16,350 --> 00:11:20,064
In 2013 the qiaopi documents
held in the provincial

174
00:11:20,144 --> 00:11:23,783
archives of Guangdong and
Fujian were inscribed on

175
00:11:23,863 --> 00:11:27,502
UNESCO's Memory of the
World register, as a record

176
00:11:27,582 --> 00:11:31,450
of Chinese migration and
of the families it divided.

177
00:11:31,450 --> 00:11:35,543
In Singapore, the National
Library holds the letters in

178
00:11:35,623 --> 00:11:40,248
the Koh Seow Chuan collection,
most of them written by Teochew

179
00:11:40,328 --> 00:11:43,966
men in Singapore between
the 1950s and the 1970s,

180
00:11:44,046 --> 00:11:48,750
and showed them in its 2012
exhibition Money by Mail to China.

181
00:11:48,750 --> 00:11:52,731
The letters also gave the
recent film Dear You its story,

182
00:11:52,811 --> 00:11:56,800
as told in the post on Dear
You and the Teochew dialect.

183
00:11:56,800 --> 00:12:01,175
The need behind the remittance
houses has not gone away.

184
00:12:01,175 --> 00:12:05,113
Many of the migrant workers who
live in Singapore today send

185
00:12:05,193 --> 00:12:09,266
part of their wages home to their
families, now through banks,

186
00:12:09,346 --> 00:12:13,351
mobile apps and remittance shops
that must be licensed by the

187
00:12:13,431 --> 00:12:17,650
Monetary Authority of Singapore
under the Payment Services Act.

188
00:12:17,650 --> 00:12:21,416
Why it matters today: The remittance
houses are easy to overlook

189
00:12:21,496 --> 00:12:25,082
because they were mostly a
sideline of tea shops and grocers,

190
00:12:25,162 --> 00:12:30,070
but for more than a century they carried
the savings of Singapore's labourers home,

191
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:32,534
and when the colonial government tried to

192
00:12:32,614 --> 00:12:36,400
take their business, they showed
how much it was worth to them.
